lessons.push({
  title: "CRUD with Python's sqlite3 and the DB-API",
  minutes: 55,
  learn: ["Connect, create a cursor, execute SQL and fetch results", "Perform Create, Read, Update and Delete safely", "Use row_factory to get dictionary-like rows", "Manage connections with context managers"],
  why: "Every database library in Python (sqlite3, MySQL and PostgreSQL drivers) follows the same standard interface, the DB-API (PEP 249). Learn it once with SQLite and you can use any database.",
  realWorld: "A contact book, an expense tracker or a student register all do exactly four things to their data: add, show, change and remove.",
  simple: "<code>connect()</code> gives a connection; <code>execute()</code> runs SQL; <code>fetchone()</code>, <code>fetchall()</code> or a loop reads rows; <code>commit()</code> saves changes.",
  detail: `<h3>The DB-API objects</h3>
${table(["Object / method", "Purpose"], [["<code>sqlite3.connect(path)</code>", "open (or create) a database; <code>\":memory:\"</code> for a temporary one"], ["<code>conn.cursor()</code>", "a handle for running statements (<code>conn.execute</code> is a shortcut)"], ["<code>cur.execute(sql, params)</code>", "run one statement with parameters"], ["<code>cur.executemany(sql, rows)</code>", "run it for many rows efficiently"], ["<code>cur.fetchone()</code>, <code>fetchall()</code>, <code>fetchmany(n)</code>, iteration", "read results"], ["<code>cur.lastrowid</code>, <code>cur.rowcount</code>", "id of the inserted row; rows changed"], ["<code>conn.commit()</code> / <code>rollback()</code>", "save or undo changes"], ["<code>conn.close()</code>", "release the connection"]])}
<h3>CRUD in SQL</h3>
${table(["Letter", "SQL", "Example"], [["Create", "INSERT", "<code>INSERT INTO t (a, b) VALUES (?, ?)</code>"], ["Read", "SELECT", "<code>SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?</code>"], ["Update", "UPDATE", "<code>UPDATE t SET b = ? WHERE id = ?</code>"], ["Delete", "DELETE", "<code>DELETE FROM t WHERE id = ?</code>"]])}
<p>Always include <code>WHERE</code> in UPDATE and DELETE unless you truly mean every row, and check <code>cur.rowcount</code> to see how many rows changed.</p>
<h3>Handy settings</h3>
<ul><li><code>conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row</code> lets you use <code>row["name"]</code> and <code>dict(row)</code>.</li><li><code>with conn:</code> wraps a transaction: commits on success, rolls back on an exception (it does <em>not</em> close the connection). Use <code>contextlib.closing(conn)</code> or call <code>close()</code> for that.</li><li><code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON</code> makes SQLite enforce foreign keys (off by default).</li></ul>`,
  syntax: { code: `conn = sqlite3.connect("app.db")\nconn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\nwith conn:\n    conn.execute("INSERT INTO t (a) VALUES (?)", (value,))\nrows = conn.execute("SELECT * FROM t WHERE a = ?", (value,)).fetchall()\nconn.close()`, note: "Pass values separately from the SQL text." },
  diagram: chain("A database call from Python", "Left to right: connect, execute SQL with parameters, fetch rows, commit the changes, close.",
    [{ k: "proc", t: "connect()" }, { k: "proc", t: "execute(sql,\nparams)" }, { k: "proc", t: "fetch rows" }, { k: "proc", t: "commit()" }, { k: "term", t: "close()" }], { w: 120, gap: 22, hgt: 52 }),
  example: { code: `import sqlite3\n\nconn = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\nconn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\nconn.execute("CREATE TABLE contacts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, phone TEXT)")\n\ncur = conn.execute("INSERT INTO contacts (name, phone) VALUES (?, ?)", ("Asha", "98765"))\nprint(cur.lastrowid)\nconn.executemany("INSERT INTO contacts (name, phone) VALUES (?, ?)", [("Ravi", "91234"), ("Zoya", "99999")])\n\nrow = conn.execute("SELECT * FROM contacts WHERE name = ?", ("Ravi",)).fetchone()\nprint(row["id"], row["name"], dict(row))\n\ncur = conn.execute("UPDATE contacts SET phone = ? WHERE name = ?", ("90000", "Ravi"))\nprint(cur.rowcount, conn.execute("SELECT phone FROM contacts WHERE id = 2").fetchone()[0])\ncur = conn.execute("DELETE FROM contacts WHERE name = ?", ("Nobody",))\nprint(cur.rowcount)\nconn.execute("DELETE FROM contacts WHERE name = ?", ("Zoya",))\nprint([r["name"] for r in conn.execute("SELECT name FROM contacts ORDER BY name")])\nconn.commit()\n\ntry:\n    with conn:\n        conn.execute("INSERT INTO contacts (name) VALUES ('Meera')")\n        conn.execute("INSERT INTO contacts (name) VALUES (NULL)")\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    print("rolled back")\nprint(conn.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM contacts").fetchone()[0])\nconn.close()`, out: `1\n2 Ravi {'id': 2, 'name': 'Ravi', 'phone': '91234'}\n1 90000\n0\n['Asha', 'Ravi']\nrolled back\n2` },
  lineByLine: [["cur.lastrowid", "The id given to the new row."], ["executemany", "One statement, several rows."], ["sqlite3.Row", "Column access by name; <code>dict(row)</code> converts it."], ["cur.rowcount", "Shows how many rows an UPDATE or DELETE touched (0 means nothing matched)."], ["with conn:", "Both inserts succeed or neither does: the NULL name makes the second fail, so Meera is rolled back."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Table contents through the example:", headers: ["After", "contacts"], rows: [["inserts", "Asha, Ravi, Zoya"], ["UPDATE Ravi", "Ravi's phone 90000"], ["DELETE Zoya", "Asha, Ravi"], ["failed transaction", "still Asha, Ravi (Meera rolled back)"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A tiny expense tracker: add rows, then summarise by category.", code: `import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE expenses (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, category TEXT, amount REAL)")\nfor category, amount in [("food", 120.5), ("travel", 300), ("food", 80), ("books", 450)]:\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO expenses (category, amount) VALUES (?, ?)", (category, amount))\nfor category, total in db.execute("SELECT category, SUM(amount) FROM expenses GROUP BY category ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC"):\n    print(f"{category:<7}{total:>8.2f}")`, out: `books    450.00\ntravel   300.00\nfood     200.50` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting <code>commit()</code> so changes vanish.", "Building SQL with f-strings (SQL injection: next lesson).", "UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE.", "Passing a single parameter without the tuple comma: <code>(value,)</code>.", "Leaving connections open."],
  practices: ["Use <code>with conn:</code> for transactions.", "Use <code>row_factory = sqlite3.Row</code> for readable code.", "Wrap database code in small functions (a data-access layer)."],
  interview: ["What is the DB-API?", "What does commit do?", "How do you read rows as dictionaries?", "Difference between fetchone and fetchall?"],
  practiceQs: ["Why is (value,) needed?", "How do you know how many rows an UPDATE changed?", "What happens to uncommitted changes when the connection closes?", "Write a function get_contact(conn, name)."],
  revision: ["connect, execute, fetch, commit, close.", "CRUD = INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE.", "Parameters with ?; one-item tuples.", "with conn: = transaction."],
  hinglish: "sqlite3 mein connect, execute, fetch aur commit ke steps hote hain. INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE aur DELETE se CRUD hota hai. Values hamesha ? se alag bhejo aur UPDATE/DELETE mein WHERE zaroor likho.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Which call saves changes to the database?", ["conn.save()", "conn.commit()", "cur.close()", "conn.flush()"], 1, "Without commit, changes are lost."),
    mcq("What does cur.rowcount give after an UPDATE?", ["The number of rows changed", "The table size", "The id", "Always 1"], 0, "0 means no row matched."),
    mcq("Which placeholder does sqlite3 use?", ["%s", "?", "$1", "{}"], 1, "Question marks (or :name)."),
    mcq("What does with conn: do on an exception?", ["Commits anyway", "Rolls back the transaction", "Closes the program", "Nothing"], 1, "The transaction is undone."),
    mcq("How do you write a single parameter tuple?", ["(value)", "(value,)", "[value]", "value"], 1, "The trailing comma makes it a tuple.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Contact book commands", "Standard input holds commands, one per line: add NAME PHONE, update NAME PHONE, delete NAME, then finally list. Use an SQLite table with a UNIQUE name. add on an existing name prints Exists; update or delete on a missing name prints Missing. At list print each contact as name phone ordered by name.", "import sqlite3, sys\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\n# create the table and process the commands\n", `import sqlite3, sys\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE contacts (name TEXT PRIMARY KEY, phone TEXT)")\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    parts = line.split()\n    if not parts:\n        continue\n    cmd = parts[0]\n    if cmd == "add":\n        try:\n            db.execute("INSERT INTO contacts VALUES (?, ?)", (parts[1], parts[2]))\n        except sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n            print("Exists")\n    elif cmd == "update":\n        if db.execute("UPDATE contacts SET phone = ? WHERE name = ?", (parts[2], parts[1])).rowcount == 0:\n            print("Missing")\n    elif cmd == "delete":\n        if db.execute("DELETE FROM contacts WHERE name = ?", (parts[1],)).rowcount == 0:\n            print("Missing")\n    elif cmd == "list":\n        for name, phone in db.execute("SELECT name, phone FROM contacts ORDER BY name"):\n            print(name, phone)`, [["add Asha 111\nadd Ravi 222\nadd Asha 333\nupdate Ravi 999\ndelete Zoya\nlist", "Exists\nMissing\nAsha 111\nRavi 999"], ["add A 1\nlist", "A 1"]], [["add Asha 111\nadd Ravi 222\nadd Asha 333\nupdate Ravi 999\ndelete Zoya\nlist", "Exists\nMissing\nAsha 111\nRavi 999"], ["add A 1\nlist", "A 1"], ["add B 2\nadd A 1\ndelete B\nlist", "A 1"], ["update X 1\nlist", "Missing"]])],
});

