lessons.push({
  title: "Parameterized Queries and SQL Injection Prevention",
  minutes: 45,
  learn: ["Explain how SQL injection works", "Always pass user input as parameters", "Know what cannot be parameterised (table and column names) and how to handle it", "Apply least privilege and validation as extra layers"],
  why: "SQL injection has been among the most damaging and common web vulnerabilities for over twenty years. Using parameters makes the whole class of attack disappear, and it takes no extra effort.",
  realWorld: "A login form where the attacker types <code>' OR '1'='1</code> as the password and gets in; a search box that lets someone dump the user table.",
  simple: "Never paste user text into SQL. Write the SQL with <strong>placeholders</strong> and hand the values over separately; the database treats them strictly as <em>data</em>, never as SQL code.",
  detail: `<h3>The attack</h3>
<p>Code such as <code>"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + name + "'"</code> turns the input <code>x' OR '1'='1</code> into <code>WHERE name = 'x' OR '1'='1'</code>, which is always true. Worse, <code>'; DROP TABLE users; --</code> may run other statements (some drivers block multiple statements, but you must not rely on that).</p>
<h3>The fix: parameters</h3>
${table(["Driver", "Placeholder style"], [["sqlite3", "<code>?</code> or <code>:name</code>"], ["MySQL connectors, psycopg", "<code>%s</code> or <code>%(name)s</code>"], ["SQLAlchemy", "bound parameters: <code>text(\"... :name\")</code>, or the expression language (automatic)"]])}
<p><code>cur.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", (name,))</code> sends the statement and the value separately. The statement is parsed first, so the value can never change its structure.</p>
<h3>What parameters cannot do</h3>
<p>You cannot parameterise <strong>identifiers</strong> (table or column names) or keywords such as <code>ASC</code>/<code>DESC</code>. Choose them from a fixed allow-list in your code: <code>column = {"name": "name", "age": "age"}[user_choice]</code>. For <code>LIKE</code> patterns, put the wildcards in the value: <code>("%" + term + "%",)</code>.</p>
<h3>Defence in depth</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Validate</strong> input (type, length, allowed characters) as well.</li><li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: the application's database user should not be able to drop tables or read tables it does not need.</li><li>Hide detailed database errors from users and log them instead.</li><li>Never store passwords in plain text: use a slow hash such as bcrypt or argon2 (Module 18).</li><li>ORMs parameterise by default, but raw SQL fragments inside an ORM can still be injectable.</li></ul>`,
  syntax: { code: `# unsafe\ncur.execute(f"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '{name}'")\n# safe\ncur.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", (name,))`, note: "Placeholders for values; allow-lists for names." },
  diagram: graph("Unsafe versus safe", "Unsafe: input is concatenated into the SQL text so it changes the query. Safe: SQL text and values travel separately and the input stays data.", 700, 200,
    [{ id: "u", k: "io", t: "user input\n' OR '1'='1", x: 100, y: 100, w: 150, h: 56 }, { id: "bad", k: "proc", t: "string concatenation\nSQL changes!", x: 400, y: 50, w: 200, h: 56 }, { id: "good", k: "proc", t: "placeholder + value\nstays data", x: 400, y: 150, w: 200, h: 56 }, { id: "x", k: "term", t: "attack succeeds", x: 590, y: 50, w: 140, h: 36 }, { id: "y", k: "term", t: "no match, no harm", x: 590, y: 150, w: 150, h: 36 }],
    [{ from: "u", to: "bad" }, { from: "u", to: "good" }, { from: "bad", to: "x" }, { from: "good", to: "y" }]),
  example: { code: `import sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE users (name TEXT, password TEXT)")\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO users VALUES ('asha', 'pw1')")\n\ndef login_unsafe(name, password):\n    sql = f"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE name = '{name}' AND password = '{password}'"\n    return db.execute(sql).fetchone()[0] > 0\n\ndef login_safe(name, password):\n    sql = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE name = ? AND password = ?"\n    return db.execute(sql, (name, password)).fetchone()[0] > 0\n\nattack = "' OR '1'='1"\nprint(login_unsafe("asha", "pw1"), login_unsafe("nobody", attack))\nprint(login_safe("asha", "pw1"), login_safe("nobody", attack))\n\ndef search(term):\n    return [r[0] for r in db.execute("SELECT name FROM users WHERE name LIKE ?", ("%" + term + "%",))]\nprint(search("sh"), search("%"))\n\nALLOWED = {"name": "name", "password": "password"}\ndef sorted_by(column):\n    return db.execute(f"SELECT name FROM users ORDER BY {ALLOWED[column]}").fetchall()\nprint(sorted_by("name"))\ntry:\n    sorted_by("name; DROP TABLE users")\nexcept KeyError:\n    print("column rejected")`, out: `True True\nTrue False\n['asha'] ['asha']\n[('asha',)]\ncolumn rejected` },
  lineByLine: [["login_unsafe", "The f-string lets the attack text rewrite the WHERE clause."], ["login_unsafe(\"nobody\", attack)", "<code>... AND password = '' OR '1'='1'</code> is true: login succeeds without a valid user."], ["login_safe", "The attack string is compared literally as a password: no match."], ["LIKE ?", "Wildcards belong in the value; user-typed % still acts as a wildcard in LIKE, so escape it if that matters."], ["ALLOWED[column]", "An allow-list is the only safe way to choose an identifier."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "What the unsafe query becomes for name = nobody, password = <code>' OR '1'='1</code>:", headers: ["Part", "Text"], rows: [["template", "... name = '{name}' AND password = '{password}'"], ["after substitution", "... name = 'nobody' AND password = '' OR '1'='1'"], ["meaning", "(false AND false) OR true = true: every row matches"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Parameters also fix quoting bugs: a name with an apostrophe breaks string-built SQL but works with placeholders.", code: `import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE people (name TEXT)")\nname = "D'Souza"\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO people VALUES (?)", (name,))\nprint(db.execute("SELECT name FROM people WHERE name = ?", (name,)).fetchone()[0])\ntry:\n    db.execute(f"INSERT INTO people VALUES ('{name}')")\nexcept sqlite3.OperationalError:\n    print("string-built SQL breaks on the apostrophe")`, out: `D'Souza\nstring-built SQL breaks on the apostrophe` },
  mistakes: ["Using f-strings, <code>%</code> or <code>+</code> to build SQL with user data.", "Thinking escaping by hand is enough.", "Trying to parameterise a table or column name.", "Showing raw database errors to users.", "Running the application as a database administrator."],
  practices: ["Parameters for every value, no exceptions.", "Allow-lists for identifiers and sort directions.", "Least-privilege database accounts."],
  interview: ["What is SQL injection and how do you prevent it?", "Can you parameterise a table name?", "Why is hand-written escaping a bad idea?", "What other layers protect a database?"],
  practiceQs: ["Rewrite a query built with an f-string to use a placeholder.", "How would you let users choose the sort column safely?", "What happens when a user types % into a LIKE search?", "Why is the apostrophe in D'Souza a security clue?"],
  revision: ["Parameters keep data and code separate.", "Placeholders: ? (sqlite3), %s (MySQL).", "Identifiers need an allow-list.", "Validate, least privilege, hide errors."],
  hinglish: "SQL injection tab hoti hai jab user ka text seedha SQL string mein jod diya jaye. Hamesha placeholder (?) use karo aur values alag se bhejo, taaki wo sirf data rahein. Table ya column ka naam placeholder se nahi jaata, uske liye allow-list banao.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What is the primary defence against SQL injection?", ["Longer passwords", "Parameterised queries", "Hiding the table names", "Using lowercase"], 1, "Values are never parsed as SQL."),
    mcq("Which can NOT be a placeholder?", ["A string value", "A number", "A table name", "A LIKE pattern value"], 2, "Identifiers must come from an allow-list."),
    mcq("What does ' OR '1'='1 do in a string-built WHERE?", ["Nothing", "Makes the condition true for every row", "Deletes the table", "Causes a syntax error always"], 1, "The OR branch is always true."),
    mcq("Which is the safe call?", ["execute(f\"... {x}\")", "execute(\"... \" + x)", "execute(\"... ?\", (x,))", "execute(\"... %s\" % x)"], 2, "Values travel separately."),
    mcq("What is least privilege?", ["Giving the app the minimum database rights it needs", "Using weak passwords", "Having one admin account", "Logging everything"], 0, "Limits the damage of any bug.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Safe login check", "A table users holds (asha, pw1) and (ravi, pw2). Read a username on the first line and a password on the second line (they may contain quotes and SQL). Use a parameterised query and print Login OK if the pair exists, otherwise Login failed.", "import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ndb.execute(\"CREATE TABLE users (name TEXT, password TEXT)\")\ndb.executemany(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)\", [(\"asha\", \"pw1\"), (\"ravi\", \"pw2\")])\nname = input()\npassword = input()\n# check with a parameterised query\n", `import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE users (name TEXT, password TEXT)")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)", [("asha", "pw1"), ("ravi", "pw2")])\nname = input()\npassword = input()\nfound = db.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE name = ? AND password = ?", (name, password)).fetchone()[0]\nprint("Login OK" if found else "Login failed")`, [["asha\npw1", "Login OK"], ["asha' --\nx", "Login failed"]], [["asha\npw1", "Login OK"], ["asha' --\nx", "Login failed"], ["' OR '1'='1\n' OR '1'='1", "Login failed"], ["ravi\npw2", "Login OK"], ["ravi\npw1", "Login failed"], ["asha\n' OR 1=1 --", "Login failed"]])],
});

