lessons.push({
  title: "Transactions: Commit, Rollback and Consistency",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Group several statements into one atomic transaction", "Use commit and rollback correctly", "Keep data consistent with constraints and foreign keys", "Understand isolation, locking and what to avoid"],
  why: "Money transfers, stock updates and bookings change several rows that must succeed or fail together. Transactions guarantee that a crash or an error never leaves half-finished data.",
  realWorld: "Moving Rs 500 from one account to another: debit and credit must both happen; booking the last seat must not sell it twice.",
  simple: "A <strong>transaction</strong> is a group of changes treated as one unit. <code>commit</code> makes them permanent; <code>rollback</code> undoes them all.",
  detail: `<h3>Transaction flow</h3>
<ol><li>Begin (implicit with the first change in the sqlite3 module).</li><li>Run the statements.</li><li>If everything is valid: <code>commit()</code>. If anything fails: <code>rollback()</code>.</li></ol>
<p>The pattern <code>with conn:</code> does this for you. In Python's sqlite3, a transaction starts automatically before INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE; check the autocommit setting of your Python version and driver and prefer explicit <code>with</code> blocks.</p>
<h3>Atomic transfer</h3>
<pre style="background:#16181c;color:#d8dee9;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>with conn:\n    conn.execute("UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - ? WHERE id = ?", (amount, src))\n    conn.execute("UPDATE acct SET bal = bal + ? WHERE id = ?", (amount, dst))</code></pre>
<p>Let the database enforce the rules: <code>CHECK (bal &gt;= 0)</code> makes an overdraft fail, and the surrounding transaction rolls back the first update.</p>
<h3>Isolation and concurrency</h3>
${table(["Problem", "What goes wrong"], [["dirty read", "reading another transaction's uncommitted change"], ["lost update", "two transactions read, change and write the same row, one overwrites the other"], ["non-repeatable read", "the same query returns different values within one transaction"], ["phantom read", "new rows appear between two identical queries"], ["deadlock", "two transactions wait for each other's locks; the database aborts one"]])}
<p>Isolation levels (read committed, repeatable read, serializable) trade safety for speed. Avoid the lost update by doing arithmetic in SQL (<code>bal = bal - ?</code>) rather than reading the value into Python, changing it and writing it back. Keep transactions <strong>short</strong>.</p>
<h3>A rollback undoes everything since the last commit</h3>
<p>If you insert your starting data and never commit, a later rollback (for example from a failed <code>with conn:</code> block) removes that data too. Commit your setup first so that a rollback only undoes the failed unit of work.</p>
<h3>Foreign keys in SQLite</h3>
<p>Run <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON</code> on every connection to make SQLite refuse orphan rows and block deletes of rows that are still referenced (or define <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>).</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `with conn:                 # commit or rollback\n    conn.execute("UPDATE ...")\n    conn.execute("UPDATE ...")\nconn.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON")`, note: "Keep transactions short." },
  diagram: graph("A transfer transaction", "Begin, debit the source, credit the destination; if both succeed commit, otherwise roll back so neither change is kept.", 640, 200,
    [{ id: "b", k: "term", t: "begin", x: 80, y: 100, w: 90, h: 36 }, { id: "d", k: "proc", t: "debit\nsource", x: 220, y: 100, w: 100, h: 50 }, { id: "c", k: "proc", t: "credit\ndestination", x: 370, y: 100, w: 120, h: 50 }, { id: "ok", k: "term", t: "commit", x: 570, y: 50, w: 90, h: 36 }, { id: "no", k: "term", t: "rollback", x: 570, y: 150, w: 100, h: 36 }],
    [{ from: "b", to: "d" }, { from: "d", to: "c" }, { from: "c", to: "ok", label: "ok" }, { from: "c", to: "no", label: "error" }]),
  example: { code: `import sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE acct (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, bal INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (bal >= 0))")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO acct VALUES (?, ?)", [(1, 100), (2, 50)])\ndb.commit()\n\ndef transfer(src, dst, amount):\n    try:\n        with db:\n            db.execute("UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - ? WHERE id = ?", (amount, src))\n            db.execute("UPDATE acct SET bal = bal + ? WHERE id = ?", (amount, dst))\n        return "ok"\n    except sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n        return "failed"\n\ndef balances():\n    return db.execute("SELECT id, bal FROM acct ORDER BY id").fetchall()\n\nprint(transfer(1, 2, 30), balances())\nprint(transfer(1, 2, 500), balances())\n\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE parent (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY)")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE child (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, parent_id INTEGER REFERENCES parent(id))")\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO parent VALUES (1)")\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO child VALUES (1, 1)")\ntry:\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO child VALUES (2, 99)")\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    print("orphan row refused")\ntry:\n    db.execute("DELETE FROM parent WHERE id = 1")\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    print("delete refused: still referenced")`, out: `ok [(1, 70), (2, 80)]\nfailed [(1, 70), (2, 80)]\norphan row refused\ndelete refused: still referenced` },
  lineByLine: [["CHECK (bal >= 0)", "The database itself refuses a negative balance."], ["with db:", "If the debit violates the check, the exception rolls back the whole block."], ["transfer(1, 2, 500)", "Fails, and the balances are exactly as before: atomicity."], ["bal = bal - ?", "The arithmetic runs inside the database, avoiding lost updates."], ["REFERENCES + foreign_keys = ON", "Orphans and dangling deletes are rejected."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "<code>transfer(1, 2, 500)</code> with balances 70 and 80:", headers: ["Step", "Effect"], rows: [["debit account 1", "70 - 500 = -430: CHECK fails, IntegrityError"], ["except path", "the with-block rolls back"], ["balances", "unchanged: 70 and 80"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Booking the last seat: do the check and the update in one SQL statement so two buyers cannot both succeed.", code: `import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE seats (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, free INTEGER)")\ndb.execute("INSERT INTO seats VALUES (1, 1)")\ndef book(seat):\n    with db:\n        return db.execute("UPDATE seats SET free = 0 WHERE id = ? AND free = 1", (seat,)).rowcount == 1\nprint(book(1), book(1))`, out: `True False` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting to commit.", "Reading a value into Python, changing it, and writing it back (lost updates).", "Long transactions that hold locks.", "Assuming SQLite enforces foreign keys by default.", "Catching an exception but continuing in a half-updated state without rollback."],
  practices: ["One logical operation = one transaction.", "Let constraints (CHECK, FOREIGN KEY, UNIQUE) catch bad data.", "Do the update conditionally in SQL."],
  interview: ["Explain ACID with a bank transfer.", "What is a lost update and how do you prevent it?", "What is a deadlock?", "Isolation levels?"],
  practiceQs: ["What happens to the first UPDATE if the second fails inside with conn:?", "Why is bal = bal - 30 better than reading bal in Python?", "What does ON DELETE CASCADE do?", "Why keep transactions short?"],
  revision: ["Transaction = atomic group; commit or rollback.", "with conn: handles it.", "Constraints enforce consistency.", "Do arithmetic in SQL; keep transactions short."],
  hinglish: "Transaction ke saare kaam ek saath hote hain ya koi nahi: commit pakka karta hai aur rollback sab undo kar deta hai. Bank transfer mein debit aur credit ek hi transaction mein rakho. bal = bal - ? SQL mein hi likho taaki lost update na ho.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does rollback do?", ["Saves changes", "Undoes the uncommitted changes of the transaction", "Closes the database", "Deletes the table"], 1, "Atomicity."),
    mcq("What stops a negative balance at the database level?", ["A CHECK constraint", "A print statement", "A comment", "An index"], 0, "The database enforces the rule."),
    mcq("What is a lost update?", ["A deleted table", "Two transactions overwrite each other's change", "A slow query", "A syntax error"], 1, "One write is silently lost."),
    mcq("Which statement avoids a lost update?", ["Read bal in Python, add, write", "UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - ?", "Sleep before writing", "Use two connections"], 1, "The database applies the arithmetic atomically."),
    mcq("Does SQLite enforce foreign keys by default?", ["Yes", "No, run PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON", "Only for integers", "Only in files"], 1, "It is off unless enabled per connection.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Bank transfers", "Read the number of accounts n and their opening balances on one line (accounts are numbered 1..n), then lines of the form transfer A B AMOUNT until the input ends. A transfer that would make the source balance negative must change nothing and print Failed; otherwise it prints OK. Finally print the balances on one line. Use an SQLite table with a CHECK constraint and a transaction.", "import sqlite3, sys\nbalances = list(map(int, input().split()))\n# create the table, then process the transfers\n", `import sqlite3, sys\nbalances = list(map(int, input().split()))\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE acct (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, bal INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (bal >= 0))")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO acct VALUES (?, ?)", list(enumerate(balances, start=1)))\ndb.commit()\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    parts = line.split()\n    if not parts:\n        continue\n    _, a, b, amount = parts\n    try:\n        with db:\n            db.execute("UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - ? WHERE id = ?", (int(amount), int(a)))\n            db.execute("UPDATE acct SET bal = bal + ? WHERE id = ?", (int(amount), int(b)))\n        print("OK")\n    except sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n        print("Failed")\nprint(" ".join(str(r[0]) for r in db.execute("SELECT bal FROM acct ORDER BY id")))`, [["100 50\ntransfer 1 2 30\ntransfer 1 2 500", "OK\nFailed\n70 80"], ["10 10\ntransfer 2 1 10", "OK\n20 0"]], [["100 50\ntransfer 1 2 30\ntransfer 1 2 500", "OK\nFailed\n70 80"], ["10 10\ntransfer 2 1 10", "OK\n20 0"], ["5\n", "5"], ["100 0 0\ntransfer 1 2 60\ntransfer 2 3 40\ntransfer 1 3 50", "OK\nOK\nFailed\n40 20 40"], ["0 0\ntransfer 1 2 1", "Failed\n0 0"]])],
});

