lessons.push({
  title: "Database Design: Relationships, Normalisation and Indexes",
  minutes: 60,
  learn: ["Model one-to-one, one-to-many and many-to-many relationships", "Normalise a table to 1NF, 2NF and 3NF", "Design keys and constraints", "Use indexes to speed queries and know their cost"],
  why: "A good schema prevents duplicate and contradictory data and makes queries simple. A bad one causes bugs that no amount of Python can fix.",
  realWorld: "A library: books, authors, members and loans; an online shop: customers, products, orders, order items.",
  simple: "Store each fact <strong>once</strong>, in the table it belongs to, and connect tables with keys. Add indexes on the columns you search by.",
  detail: `<h3>Relationships</h3>
${table(["Kind", "How to model", "Example"], [["one-to-one", "foreign key with UNIQUE (or share the primary key)", "user and profile"], ["one-to-many", "foreign key on the &quot;many&quot; side", "one customer, many orders (<code>orders.customer_id</code>)"], ["many-to-many", "a <strong>junction table</strong> holding two foreign keys", "students and courses via <code>enrolments</code>"]])}
<h3>Normalisation</h3>
${table(["Form", "Rule", "Violation example"], [["1NF", "each cell holds one atomic value; no repeating groups", "a column <code>phones = '111,222'</code>"], ["2NF", "every non-key column depends on the <em>whole</em> key (matters for composite keys)", "<code>(student, course) -> student_name</code>"], ["3NF", "non-key columns depend only on the key, not on other non-key columns", "<code>employee(dept_id, dept_name)</code>: dept_name depends on dept_id"]])}
<p>Normalising removes <strong>update anomalies</strong> (change a department name in one row but not another), insert anomalies and delete anomalies. Sometimes data is <em>denormalised</em> on purpose for read speed (reports, caches), but know that you are trading consistency for speed.</p>
<h3>Keys</h3>
<ul><li>Prefer a <strong>surrogate key</strong> (<code>id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code>) for tables whose natural data can change; add <code>UNIQUE</code> on natural identifiers such as email.</li><li>Use <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>CHECK</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code> and <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> constraints.</li></ul>
<h3>Indexes</h3>
<p>An index (a B-tree) lets the database find rows without scanning the whole table: lookups drop from O(n) to O(log n). Index the columns used in <code>WHERE</code>, <code>JOIN ... ON</code> and <code>ORDER BY</code>. The costs: extra disk space and slower writes. Check with <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> (SQLite) whether an index is used. Primary keys and UNIQUE columns are indexed automatically.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `CREATE TABLE enrolments (\n    student_id INTEGER REFERENCES students(id),\n    course_id  INTEGER REFERENCES courses(id),\n    PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)\n);\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders(customer_id);`, lang: "sql" },
  diagram: ((() => { const inner = entity(20, 30, 170, "students", ["id  PK", "name"]) + entity(270, 30, 220, "enrolments", ["student_id  FK", "course_id  FK", "grade"]) + entity(570, 30, 170, "courses", ["id  PK", "title"]) + `<line x1="190" y1="70" x2="270" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><line x1="570" y1="70" x2="490" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#ah)"/>` + tx(105, 130, "1", 14) + tx(250, 62, "N", 13) + tx(515, 62, "N", 13) + tx(655, 130, "1", 14) + tx(380, 170, "many-to-many through a junction table", 12); return svgWrap(760, 190, "Many-to-many relationship", "Students and courses are linked many-to-many through the enrolments junction table with two foreign keys.", inner); })()),
  example: { code: `import sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON")\ndb.executescript("""\nCREATE TABLE students (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, email TEXT UNIQUE);\nCREATE TABLE courses (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL);\nCREATE TABLE enrolments (\n    student_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES students(id),\n    course_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES courses(id),\n    PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)\n);\nINSERT INTO students (name, email) VALUES ('Asha', 'a@x.in'), ('Ravi', 'r@x.in'), ('Zoya', 'z@x.in');\nINSERT INTO courses (title) VALUES ('Python'), ('SQL');\nINSERT INTO enrolments VALUES (1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 1), (3, 2);\n""")\nprint(db.execute("""SELECT s.name, c.title FROM enrolments e\n    JOIN students s ON s.id = e.student_id JOIN courses c ON c.id = e.course_id\n    ORDER BY s.name, c.title""").fetchall())\nprint(db.execute("""SELECT c.title, COUNT(*) FROM enrolments e JOIN courses c ON c.id = e.course_id\n    GROUP BY c.id ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, c.title""").fetchall())\ntry:\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO enrolments VALUES (1, 1)")\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    print("already enrolled")\ntry:\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO students (name, email) VALUES ('Dup', 'a@x.in')")\nexcept sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n    print("email must be unique")\n\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE big (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, k INTEGER)")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO big (k) VALUES (?)", [(i % 1000,) for i in range(5000)])\nplan_before = " ".join(str(r) for r in db.execute("EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT * FROM big WHERE k = 7"))\ndb.execute("CREATE INDEX idx_big_k ON big(k)")\nplan_after = " ".join(str(r) for r in db.execute("EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT * FROM big WHERE k = 7"))\nprint("SCAN" in plan_before, "idx_big_k" in plan_after)`, out: `[('Asha', 'Python'), ('Asha', 'SQL'), ('Ravi', 'Python'), ('Zoya', 'SQL')]\n[('Python', 2), ('SQL', 2)]\nalready enrolled\nemail must be unique\nTrue True` },
  lineByLine: [["junction table enrolments", "Each row is one student-in-one-course; the composite primary key prevents duplicates."], ["two JOINs", "Reassemble the readable pairs from the three tables."], ["UNIQUE on email", "Stops two students sharing an email."], ["EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN", "Before the index the query scans the table; after CREATE INDEX it searches the index."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Why 3NF matters: update the department name once.", headers: ["Design", "Rows to change when Computer Science is renamed"], rows: [["dept_name copied into every employee row", "every employee of the department (and a typo creates two names)"], ["separate departments table + dept_id", "one row"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A normalised order schema answers a business question with one join.", code: `import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.executescript("""\nCREATE TABLE products (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, price INTEGER);\nCREATE TABLE orders (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE order_items (order_id INTEGER, product_id INTEGER, qty INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO products VALUES (1, 'pen', 10), (2, 'bag', 800);\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES (1, 'Asha'), (2, 'Ravi');\nINSERT INTO order_items VALUES (1, 1, 5), (1, 2, 1), (2, 1, 2);\n""")\nprint(db.execute("""SELECT o.customer, SUM(p.price * i.qty) FROM orders o\n    JOIN order_items i ON i.order_id = o.id JOIN products p ON p.id = i.product_id\n    GROUP BY o.id ORDER BY o.customer""").fetchall())`, out: `[('Asha', 850), ('Ravi', 20)]` },
  mistakes: ["Storing lists in one column.", "Copying the same fact into many rows.", "Missing the junction table for many-to-many.", "Indexing every column (slows writes).", "No foreign key constraints."],
  practices: ["Draw the entities and relationships first.", "Normalise to 3NF, then denormalise only with a reason.", "Add indexes for the queries you actually run."],
  interview: ["Explain 1NF, 2NF and 3NF.", "How do you model many-to-many?", "What is an index and what does it cost?", "What is denormalisation?"],
  practiceQs: ["Design tables for a library (books, members, loans).", "Which column would you index in orders for 'orders of customer X'?", "Why does a primary key not need an extra index?", "What anomaly does 3NF remove?"],
  revision: ["1-1, 1-N (FK on many side), M-N (junction table).", "1NF atomic, 2NF whole key, 3NF only the key.", "Constraints enforce rules.", "Indexes speed reads, slow writes."],
  hinglish: "Har fact ko ek hi jagah store karo aur tables ko keys se jodo. Many-to-many ke liye beech mein junction table banao. Normalisation duplicate data aur update galtiyan rokta hai, aur jis column par search karte ho us par index lagao.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("How is a many-to-many relationship modelled?", ["One table", "A junction table with two foreign keys", "A list column", "A view only"], 1, "Each row links one pair."),
    mcq("Which normal form requires atomic values in every cell?", ["1NF", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF only"], 0, "No repeating groups or lists in a cell."),
    mcq("What is a downside of indexes?", ["They slow reads", "They use space and slow writes", "They remove constraints", "They sort tables"], 1, "Every write must update the index."),
    mcq("Where does the foreign key go in a one-to-many relationship?", ["On the 'one' side", "On the 'many' side", "In both", "Nowhere"], 1, "orders.customer_id."),
    mcq("What does 3NF forbid?", ["Non-key columns depending on other non-key columns", "Primary keys", "Foreign keys", "Joins"], 0, "A transitive dependency.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Students per course", "Read n, then n lines student course (an enrolment). Store them in SQLite tables students(id, name UNIQUE), courses(id, title UNIQUE) and enrolments (a junction table), then print each course and its number of distinct students as title count, ordered by count descending and then title.", "import sqlite3\nn = int(input())\n# create the three tables, insert the enrolments, and query\n", `import sqlite3\nn = int(input())\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.executescript("""\nCREATE TABLE students (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT UNIQUE);\nCREATE TABLE courses (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT UNIQUE);\nCREATE TABLE enrolments (student_id INTEGER, course_id INTEGER, PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id));\n""")\nfor _ in range(n):\n    student, course = input().split()\n    db.execute("INSERT OR IGNORE INTO students (name) VALUES (?)", (student,))\n    db.execute("INSERT OR IGNORE INTO courses (title) VALUES (?)", (course,))\n    db.execute("""INSERT OR IGNORE INTO enrolments SELECT s.id, c.id FROM students s, courses c WHERE s.name = ? AND c.title = ?""", (student, course))\nfor title, count in db.execute("""SELECT c.title, COUNT(*) FROM enrolments e JOIN courses c ON c.id = e.course_id GROUP BY c.id ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, c.title"""):\n    print(title, count)`, [["4\nAsha Python\nAsha SQL\nRavi Python\nAsha Python", "Python 2\nSQL 1"], ["1\nZoya Java", "Java 1"]], [["4\nAsha Python\nAsha SQL\nRavi Python\nAsha Python", "Python 2\nSQL 1"], ["1\nZoya Java", "Java 1"], ["3\nA X\nB X\nC Y", "X 2\nY 1"], ["4\nA P\nB Q\nC P\nD Q", "P 2\nQ 2"]])],
});

