lessons.push({
  title: "ORM Introduction and SQLAlchemy",
  minutes: 60,
  learn: ["Explain what an object-relational mapper does and its trade-offs", "Define models and relationships with SQLAlchemy 2.0", "Create, query, update and delete with a Session", "Avoid the N+1 query problem and know when to use raw SQL"],
  why: "An ORM lets you work with Python objects instead of hand-written SQL strings and handles parameter binding for you. SQLAlchemy is the most widely used Python ORM and the foundation of many frameworks.",
  realWorld: "FastAPI and Flask applications define <code>User</code> and <code>Order</code> classes and read and write them through SQLAlchemy sessions.",
  simple: "A <strong>model</strong> class describes a table; an <strong>instance</strong> is a row. You <code>add</code> objects to a <strong>session</strong> and <code>commit</code>; queries return objects instead of tuples.",
  detail: `<p><strong>Requires:</strong> <code>python -m pip install "sqlalchemy&gt;=2.0"</code> (examples were run with SQLAlchemy 2.0). CI and the course checks install it.</p>
<h3>Core pieces (SQLAlchemy 2.0 style)</h3>
${table(["Piece", "Role"], [["<code>create_engine(url)</code>", "the connection factory and pool; the URL names the database (<code>sqlite://</code>, <code>mysql+pymysql://...</code>, <code>postgresql+psycopg://...</code>)"], ["<code>DeclarativeBase</code>, <code>Mapped[...]</code>, <code>mapped_column</code>", "define models with type hints"], ["<code>relationship()</code>", "navigate between related objects (<code>author.books</code>)"], ["<code>Session</code>", "unit of work: tracks changes and runs a transaction"], ["<code>select(Model)</code>", "build queries; <code>session.scalars(...)</code> returns objects"], ["Alembic", "database migrations (schema changes tracked in code)"]])}
<h3>The unit of work</h3>
<p>Changes to objects are collected by the session and sent as SQL on <code>flush</code>/<code>commit</code>. Wrap work in <code>with Session(engine) as session, session.begin():</code> so it commits or rolls back for you.</p>
<h3>The N+1 problem</h3>
<p>Looping over 100 authors and touching <code>author.books</code> for each can fire 101 queries (one for authors plus one per author). Fix with eager loading: <code>select(Author).options(selectinload(Author.books))</code> (2 queries) or a JOIN.</p>
<h3>ORM or raw SQL?</h3>
<ul><li>ORM: faster to write, safer against injection, portable across databases, easy relationships.</li><li>Raw SQL / SQLAlchemy Core: complex reports, bulk operations, performance-critical queries. You can mix them.</li><li>Learn SQL first: you will still need it to understand what the ORM sends.</li></ul>`,
  syntax: { code: `class Base(DeclarativeBase): pass\nclass Student(Base):\n    __tablename__ = "students"\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    name: Mapped[str]\nwith Session(engine) as s, s.begin():\n    s.add(Student(name="Asha"))`, note: "A model class is a table; an instance is a row." },
  diagram: chain("ORM layers", "Left to right: Python objects, the SQLAlchemy session and unit of work, generated SQL, the database.",
    [{ k: "proc", t: "Python objects\n(models)" }, { k: "proc", t: "Session\n(unit of work)" }, { k: "proc", t: "generated SQL\n(parameterised)" }, { k: "term", t: "database" }], { w: 140, gap: 30, hgt: 58 }),
  example: { code: `from sqlalchemy import ForeignKey, create_engine, select, func, event\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, Mapped, mapped_column, relationship, Session, selectinload\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase):\n    pass\n\nclass Author(Base):\n    __tablename__ = "authors"\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    name: Mapped[str]\n    books: Mapped[list["Book"]] = relationship(back_populates="author")\n\nclass Book(Base):\n    __tablename__ = "books"\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    title: Mapped[str]\n    year: Mapped[int]\n    author_id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(ForeignKey("authors.id"))\n    author: Mapped[Author] = relationship(back_populates="books")\n\nengine = create_engine("sqlite://")\nBase.metadata.create_all(engine)\nqueries = []\n@event.listens_for(engine, "before_cursor_execute")\ndef count_queries(conn, cursor, statement, *args):\n    queries.append(statement)\n\nwith Session(engine) as session, session.begin():\n    asha = Author(name="Asha", books=[Book(title="Python 101", year=2020), Book(title="SQL Basics", year=2022)])\n    ravi = Author(name="Ravi", books=[Book(title="Data Tools", year=2024)])\n    session.add_all([asha, ravi])\n\nwith Session(engine) as session:\n    recent = session.scalars(select(Book).where(Book.year >= 2022).order_by(Book.title)).all()\n    print([b.title for b in recent])\n    print(session.scalar(select(func.count(Book.id))))\n    queries.clear()\n    for author in session.scalars(select(Author).order_by(Author.name)):\n        _ = len(author.books)\n    lazy = len(queries)\n    queries.clear()\n    for author in session.scalars(select(Author).options(selectinload(Author.books)).order_by(Author.name)):\n        _ = len(author.books)\n    print(lazy, len(queries))\n\nwith Session(engine) as session, session.begin():\n    book = session.scalars(select(Book).where(Book.title == "Data Tools")).one()\n    book.year = 2025\n    session.delete(session.scalars(select(Book).where(Book.title == "SQL Basics")).one())\nwith Session(engine) as session:\n    print([(b.title, b.year, b.author.name) for b in session.scalars(select(Book).order_by(Book.id))])`, out: `['Data Tools', 'SQL Basics']\n3\n3 2\n[('Python 101', 2020, 'Asha'), ('Data Tools', 2025, 'Ravi')]` },
  lineByLine: [["class Author(Base)", "A table described as a Python class with typed columns."], ["relationship(back_populates=...)", "Lets you move between an author and their books as ordinary attributes."], ["session.begin()", "Commits on success, rolls back on error."], ["select(Book).where(...).order_by(...)", "A query built from expressions: values are bound as parameters automatically."], ["lazy vs selectinload", "Touching author.books inside the loop issues one extra query per author (1 + 2 = 3); selectinload loads all books in one extra query (2 total)."], ["book.year = 2025", "Changing an attribute is enough: the session sends the UPDATE at commit."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Queries issued by the two loops (2 authors):", headers: ["Style", "Queries"], rows: [["lazy loading", "1 for authors + 1 per author = 3"], ["selectinload", "1 for authors + 1 for all their books = 2"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "The same data with plain SQL for comparison: the ORM generates statements like this.", code: `import sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.executescript("CREATE TABLE books (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT, year INTEGER); INSERT INTO books (title, year) VALUES ('Python 101', 2020), ('SQL Basics', 2022);")\nprint(db.execute("SELECT title FROM books WHERE year >= ? ORDER BY title", (2022,)).fetchall())`, out: `[('SQL Basics',)]` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting to commit (or use <code>session.begin()</code>).", "The N+1 problem from lazy loading in loops.", "Using a session after it is closed or sharing one session between threads.", "Following 1.x tutorials (legacy <code>Query</code> API, <code>declarative_base()</code>) and mixing styles.", "Building SQL strings inside text() with f-strings."],
  practices: ["One session per unit of work (per request).", "Eager-load relationships you will use in a loop.", "Use migrations (Alembic) for schema changes in real projects.", "Read the SQL the ORM emits (<code>echo=True</code>) while learning."],
  interview: ["What is an ORM and what are its drawbacks?", "What is the N+1 problem?", "Session vs engine vs connection?", "When would you use raw SQL instead?"],
  practiceQs: ["Which URL connects SQLAlchemy to an in-memory SQLite database?", "How do you avoid N+1 queries?", "What does session.add_all do?", "How would you add a new column to a table in production?"],
  revision: ["Engine, models, Session, select().", "Instances are rows; attributes are columns.", "Relationships and eager loading.", "ORM for convenience, SQL for complex reports."],
  hinglish: "ORM mein table ek class hoti hai aur row uska object. Session badlaav ikatthe karke commit par SQL bhejta hai. Loop mein relationship chhune se N+1 queries ban sakti hain, isliye selectinload jaisa eager loading use karo. SQL pehle seekho, ORM baad mein.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does an ORM map?", ["Tables to objects", "Files to folders", "Threads to processes", "Functions to modules"], 0, "Rows become Python objects."),
    mcq("What is the N+1 problem?", ["One query per related object in a loop", "A syntax error", "A missing index", "A slow import"], 0, "1 query for the parents plus N for the children."),
    mcq("How do you fix N+1 in SQLAlchemy?", ["More loops", "Eager loading such as selectinload", "A bigger server", "Sleep"], 1, "Load the related rows in one extra query."),
    mcq("What does create_engine(\"sqlite://\") create?", ["An in-memory SQLite engine", "A MySQL connection", "A file named sqlite", "A table"], 0, "No path means an in-memory database."),
    mcq("What sends the pending changes to the database?", ["session.commit() / flush", "print()", "import", "session.close() only"], 0, "The unit of work flushes at commit.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Map rows to objects", "Read n, then n lines id name price. Store them in an SQLite table, read them back through sqlite3.Row into a dataclass Product, and print the names of the products whose price is above the average price, from the most expensive to the cheapest. If none is above the average print None.", "import sqlite3\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Product:\n    id: int\n    name: str\n    price: float\n\nn = int(input())\n# store the rows, map them to Product objects, print the expensive ones\n", `import sqlite3\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Product:\n    id: int\n    name: str\n    price: float\n\nn = int(input())\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE products (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, price REAL)")\nfor _ in range(n):\n    pid, name, price = input().split()\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO products VALUES (?, ?, ?)", (int(pid), name, float(price)))\nproducts = [Product(**dict(row)) for row in db.execute("SELECT * FROM products")]\naverage = sum(p.price for p in products) / len(products)\nexpensive = sorted((p for p in products if p.price > average), key=lambda p: -p.price)\nprint(" ".join(p.name for p in expensive) if expensive else "None")`, [["3\n1 pen 10\n2 bag 800\n3 book 250", "bag"], ["2\n1 a 5\n2 b 5", "None"]], [["3\n1 pen 10\n2 bag 800\n3 book 250", "bag"], ["2\n1 a 5\n2 b 5", "None"], ["4\n1 a 1\n2 b 2\n3 c 3\n4 d 10", "d"], ["3\n1 x 10\n2 y 20\n3 z 30", "z"], ["4\n1 a 10\n2 b 20\n3 c 30\n4 d 40", "d c"]])],
});

module.exports = {
  module: {
    title: "Phase 4 - Database Programming",
    description: "Database fundamentals, SQL revision, SQLite CRUD with the DB-API, parameterised queries and SQL injection prevention, transactions, schema design with relationships and indexes, MySQL connectivity and an introduction to ORMs with SQLAlchemy.",
    chapters: [{ title: "Database Programming", description: "From tables and SQL to safe, transactional Python database code.", lessons }],
    assessment: {
      title: "Module 16 Assessment: Database Programming",
      description: "SQLite problems: aggregation, joins, safe search and transactions.",
      questions: [
        task("Top earner per department", "Read n, then n lines name dept salary. Using SQL, print for every department the name of its highest-paid person (if tied, the alphabetically first name) as dept name, ordered by department.", "", `import sqlite3\nn = int(input())\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE staff (name TEXT, dept TEXT, salary INTEGER)")\nfor _ in range(n):\n    name, dept, salary = input().split()\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO staff VALUES (?, ?, ?)", (name, dept, int(salary)))\nfor dept, name in db.execute("""SELECT dept, MIN(name) FROM staff s WHERE salary = (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM staff WHERE dept = s.dept) GROUP BY dept ORDER BY dept"""):\n    print(dept, name)`, [["5\nAsha CSE 90\nRavi CSE 70\nZoya ECE 80\nKiran ECE 80\nMeera CSE 90", "CSE Asha\nECE Kiran"], ["1\nA X 1", "X A"]], [["5\nAsha CSE 90\nRavi CSE 70\nZoya ECE 80\nKiran ECE 80\nMeera CSE 90", "CSE Asha\nECE Kiran"], ["1\nA X 1", "X A"], ["3\nb P 5\na P 9\nc Q 1", "P a\nQ c"], ["2\nx M 7\ny M 7", "M x"]]),
        task("Overdue books", "Read n, then n lines book member due_day (an integer day number), then today's day number. Print the books that are overdue (due_day is less than today), ordered by book name, as book member days_late. Print None if nothing is overdue. Use SQL.", "", `import sqlite3\nn = int(input())\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE loans (book TEXT, member TEXT, due INTEGER)")\nfor _ in range(n):\n    book, member, due = input().split()\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO loans VALUES (?, ?, ?)", (book, member, int(due)))\ntoday = int(input())\nrows = db.execute("SELECT book, member, ? - due FROM loans WHERE due < ? ORDER BY book", (today, today)).fetchall()\nprint("\\n".join(f"{b} {m} {d}" for b, m, d in rows) if rows else "None")`, [["3\nPython Asha 10\nSQL Ravi 20\nJava Zoya 5\n12", "Java Zoya 7\nPython Asha 2"], ["1\nA B 5\n5", "None"]], [["3\nPython Asha 10\nSQL Ravi 20\nJava Zoya 5\n12", "Java Zoya 7\nPython Asha 2"], ["1\nA B 5\n5", "None"], ["2\nX Y 1\nZ W 2\n3", "X Y 2\nZ W 1"], ["1\nBook Me 100\n50", "None"]]),
        task("Safe name search", "A table people holds the names given on the first line (separated by spaces; one name per row). Read a search term on the second line and, with a parameterised LIKE query, print the names that contain the term (case-insensitive), ordered alphabetically, one per line, or None. The term may contain quotes.", "", `import sqlite3\nnames = input().split()\nterm = input()\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE people (name TEXT)")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO people VALUES (?)", [(n,) for n in names])\nrows = [r[0] for r in db.execute("SELECT name FROM people WHERE name LIKE ? ORDER BY name", ("%" + term + "%",))]\nprint("\\n".join(rows) if rows else "None")`, [["Asha Ravi Zoya Kiran\nra", "Kiran\nRavi"], ["Asha Ravi\n' OR '1'='1", "None"]], [["Asha Ravi Zoya Kiran\nra", "Kiran\nRavi"], ["Asha Ravi\n' OR '1'='1", "None"], ["Asha Ashok Ravi\nas", "Asha\nAshok"], ["Asha\nx", "None"]]),
        task("Inventory with a transaction", "Read the initial stock (item and quantity pairs) on the first line like pen 5 bag 2, then lines of the form buy ITEM QTY. A purchase is refused (print Refused and change nothing) if the item is unknown or the stock is too low; otherwise print OK. Use an SQLite table with a CHECK constraint and a transaction. Finally print the stock of each item as item qty ordered by item.", "", `import sqlite3, sys\npairs = input().split()\ndb = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\ndb.execute("CREATE TABLE stock (item TEXT PRIMARY KEY, qty INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (qty >= 0))")\ndb.executemany("INSERT INTO stock VALUES (?, ?)", [(pairs[i], int(pairs[i + 1])) for i in range(0, len(pairs), 2)])\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    parts = line.split()\n    if not parts:\n        continue\n    _, item, qty = parts\n    try:\n        with db:\n            changed = db.execute("UPDATE stock SET qty = qty - ? WHERE item = ?", (int(qty), item)).rowcount\n            if changed == 0:\n                raise sqlite3.IntegrityError("unknown item")\n        print("OK")\n    except sqlite3.IntegrityError:\n        print("Refused")\nfor item, qty in db.execute("SELECT item, qty FROM stock ORDER BY item"):\n    print(item, qty)`, [["pen 5 bag 2\nbuy pen 3\nbuy bag 5\nbuy ink 1", "OK\nRefused\nRefused\nbag 2\npen 2"], ["a 1\nbuy a 1", "OK\na 0"]], [["pen 5 bag 2\nbuy pen 3\nbuy bag 5\nbuy ink 1", "OK\nRefused\nRefused\nbag 2\npen 2"], ["a 1\nbuy a 1", "OK\na 0"], ["x 10 y 10\nbuy x 10\nbuy y 11\nbuy x 1", "OK\nRefused\nRefused\nx 0\ny 10"], ["p 3", "p 3"]]),
      ],
    },
  },
};
